[Music]  this morning a special edition of the  dish with Bruce and Eric Bromberg their  Blue Ribbon restaurant Empire got its  start 25 years ago this fall with the  original Blue Ribbon Brasserie here in  New York considered a cradle of today's  culinary scene it was a hangout for some  then-unknown chefs who would go on to  become some of the biggest restaurant  tours in the world I recently sat down  with the founders and tasted the  timeless appeal of Blue Ribbon all right  so this is a little trifecta of  definitely fun food at blue you're  definitely using some fingers and kind  of having fun from fondue to pupu  platter  to their famous fried chicken Eric and  Bruce Bromberg say the secret to Blue  Ribbon is a brotherly love of eating  ever since we were young  ribs were just like always the highlight  for me so it's just a situation of let's  make things that we love there you go  the chef's behind this finger food were  actually classically trained at La  Cordon Bleu in Paris but they found  cooking in three-star restaurants far  from satisfying one of the things that  really struck me was none of my friends  could come to those restaurants they  were so expensive so exclusive but why  don't we just take the technique of fine  cooking let's apply it to everything we  learned and let's make it accessible to  everyone they opened the Blue Ribbon  Brasserie on Sullivan Street in Soho on  November 3rd 1992 Kimball Cramer showed  up just a few days later he's been  coming back up to five times a week ever  since I saw a new restaurant walked in  and liked it  food was outstanding I was looking for a  hangout place and I knew I had found it  it all began in this you know tiny  little hole in the wall this was two  apartments that we joined together and  you know kind of had this idea of just  making a good restaurant they started  with a simple notion what is blumerman  its first prize it's the best things it  sounded American the translation of  cordon bleu is Blue Ribbon so it all  kind of came together it was more of an  idea and I think that's what Blue Ribbon  meant to us it was this concept it was  this heart and soul of everything we had  done growing up as kids you used to  actually play restaurant  we played restaurant but we pretty much  just played Benihana was back in the  Benihana that hold constant being and  seeing a chef in front of us was just  the coolest thing in the world and we'd  always try to order shrimp cocktail  which our dad never let us do and it has  been a special at Blue Ribbon for 25  years just because of the special every  single by the Bromberg's credit their  father an attorney for their insatiable  appetite it was always about that next  meal summer camp in Maine there were  stops along the way where it was you  know pizzas in New Haven and clam  chowder at this place and the lobster at  this place  where are we going in Chinatown in New  York where do you get the best egg roll  where do you get the best fun do you  know like everything was about the meal  but as they were getting ready to open  Blue Ribbon  there was one key ingredient missing we  didn't really know what the menu was  gonna be and at one point literally a  couple days before opening Eric's wife  Ellen was like guys we need to print  menus we need like lists you know what  are you doing Blue Ribbon which stayed  open till 4:00 in the morning would  become a late-night hangout for  up-and-coming chefs coming off their  shifts it was that time when the entire  culinary scene was giving birth in a  sense and the group of people whether it  was Danielle and his team or Bobby and  his team Mario Mario was the sous chef  at a local Italian place he had just  moved from Sea  and he was come in every single night  and sit at the bar and we all just hung  out this dish I think resonated with the  chef world more than any other dish it's  the beef marrow and oxtail marmalade  that they would eat in Paris they call  them Oh some wall and we would look at  each other and be like this is  ridiculous it's better than foie gras  but we had never heard of it before we  hadn't seen it in any books delicious  it's crazy it's one of those wishes it  should never be messed with it yeah we  may never make just sitting here again  and thinking of how many different  people have sat at this table over the  years from you know President Clinton to  Mick Jagger to this chefs that we grew  up learning about NIDA lysing Danielle  or Sean George or any of these people  coming in and saying like this is the  greatest place I've ever been to and  we're like it wasn't like this I've ever  had right it wasn't that it was that  they saw something different in blue  ribbon there was something different  going on here that's something different  helped the Bromberg's  turn blue ribbon Brasserie into the blue  ribbon Empire operating 20 restaurants  from fried chicken to sushi  around the country over the last 25  years so is this more fun than the  Benihana is kids having this this is  more adult fun eating like this is I  think ultimately what makes us really  happy and thankfully it makes some other  people happy too started as a family  affair still is and you can taste the  love in their food I love that they  played restaurant as killer Lee they  played Benihana

